          </h2>
        </header>
        <div class="case-reading case-copy" data-case-reveal>
          <p>
            The first question was which relationships a PDF parser could
            preserve and which needed further work. I tested MinerU, a
            structured PDF parser, against merged cells, a table split across
            pages, and an interrupted paragraph. It recovered those structures
            in the test document, giving the pipeline a useful starting point
            for text and tables.
          </p>
          <p>
            A reference such as Note (4) needed a different repair. Recognising
            the sentence and the note as text still left them several pages
            apart, where chunking could separate them. Their explicit reference
            gave me a relationship I could reconstruct and check. I wrote a
            rule-based linker to locate the matching note, repair splits in its
            text, and place it beside the sentence that cited it.
          </p>
          <p>
            In a flowchart, the words acquire meaning through the arrows and
            branches between them. OCR could recover the labels while losing the
            decision path. I compared it with descriptions and Mermaid diagrams
            produced by a vision-language model (VLM), which can interpret both
            images and text. Mermaid records nodes, labels, and branches in a
            text-based diagram format. In the controlled test, it preserved the
            complete flowchart and supported correct answers to both questions
            about it.
          </p>
          <p>
            These results supported a pipeline in which MinerU handled text and
            tables, explicit references were repaired by rules, and the VLM
            reconstructed flowcharts. The repaired material then entered RAGFlow
