  name: "RagflowPipelinePage",
  components: {
    ProjectChapterNav,
    ProjectPageHeader,
    ReferenceLinkingDemo,
    FlowchartReconstructionDemo,
  },
  data() {
    return {
      openStage: null,
      openResearch: null,
      linked: false,
      stages: [
        {
          key: "pdf",
          name: "Test document and acceptance criteria",
          body: "I made a five-page document that gathered the known failure cases in one place. It contained merged cells, a table and a paragraph interrupted by page breaks, notes defined several pages after their citations, and a flowchart. Because the input stayed fixed, I could repeat the same questions after each change and see whether one repair had damaged another part of the document.",
        },
        {
          key: "mineru",
          name: "PDF parsing with MinerU",
          body: "MinerU converted the PDF into structured text while retaining much of its layout. In the test document, it preserved cells spanning several rows or columns, reconnected a table divided by a page boundary, and restored an interrupted paragraph. I could then concentrate the custom code on the relationships it still missed.",
        },
        {
          key: "notes",
          name: "Cross-page reference linking",
          body: "Some sentences ended with a reference such as (4), while the corresponding Note (4) appeared several pages later. I wrote a rule-based linker that located the matching note, repaired cases where the parser had split it, and copied it beside the sentence that cited it. Removing the standalone duplicate left the rule and its qualification together for chunking.",
          rules: [
            "Detect a citation such as (4) at the end of a sentence.",
            "Locate the matching Note (4) or Remark (4) elsewhere in the relevant section.",
            "Repair cases where the parser has divided one note into multiple fragments.",
            "Normalise small inconsistencies in the parsed note labels.",
            "Copy the note beside the sentence that cites it, then remove the duplicated standalone copy.",
          ],
        },
        {
          key: "mermaid",
          name: "Flowchart reconstruction with VLM and Mermaid",
          body: "Optical character recognition (OCR) could read the words inside a flowchart, yet the answer often depended on which branch connected them. I asked a vision-language model (VLM) to reconstruct the diagram as Mermaid, recording its nodes, arrows, labels, and branches in text. That representation could be searched alongside the rest of the document, and I could render it back into a diagram to inspect whether the logic had survived.",
        },
        {
          key: "chunking",
          name: "Chunking into self-contained evidence units",
          body: "RAGFlow divided the repaired document into the small passages it would later search. By this point, each cross-page note had been placed beside the sentence it qualified. The rule and the conditions governing it could therefore travel through retrieval as one unit of evidence.",
        },
        {
          key: "embedding",
          name: "Embedding for semantic retrieval",
          body: "The same embedding model represented each chunk and the question as numerical vectors. Comparing those vectors let the system retrieve passages with related meanings even when their wording differed. Mermaid entered this stage as text, making the flowchart’s decision logic searchable alongside paragraphs and tables.",
        },
        {
          key: "retrieval",
          name: "Retrieving the evidence for a question",
          body: "In the controlled test, the passage containing both the sentence and its linked note became the top result for the question it answered. The relationship had survived parsing, repair, and search, and was still available at the moment the system assembled evidence for the answer.",
        },
        {
          key: "generation",
          name: "Writing the answer from the retrieved evidence",
          body: "Finally, the system gave the retrieved passages to DeepSeek V4-flash, the language model used in the proof of concept. With the rule and its qualification available together, it had the evidence needed to explain when the rule applied.",
        },
      ],
      research: [
        {
          title: "Multi-page OCR with Baidu",
          body: "Baidu Unlimited OCR offered a multi-page mode, so I tested whether reading several pages together would preserve relationships across them. Page layout survived reasonably well on difficult public government documents. The link between a rule and a later note still had to be rebuilt explicitly. Its available documentation also described a lower-detail configuration trained on pages joined together, which mattered when weighing the apparent convenience against the evidence it could preserve.",
        },
        {
          title: "Embedding pages as images",
          body: "I tested image-based and multimodal alternatives because page images preserve visual layout. Processing each page independently still separated cross-page notes from their citations. In the RAGFlow path I evaluated, a vision model converted the image to text, which was then embedded. Reconstructing the relationships explicitly in that text gave more control over which pieces retrieval could return together.",
        },
        {
          title: "Three ways to make flowcharts searchable",
          body: "I compared OCR, free-form descriptions from a vision-language model, and Mermaid reconstructed by the same model. OCR captured the words without the arrows or reading order. Free-form descriptions changed their level of detail from one figure to another. In the controlled test, Mermaid preserved every node, edge, and label and supported correct answers to both flowchart questions.",
        },
        {
          title: "What the tests changed in the design",
          body: "Tables and paragraphs needed repair before chunking, while their relationships could still be recovered. Repeated note numbers also meant that a number alone could not identify the right note, so the linker used position and surrounding context. I checked the final chunks for the rule and its qualification together, because that was the evidence an answer would depend on.",
        },
      ],
    };
  },
};
</script>
